// The guarantees: what star-burxt accepts, and what it refuses and why.
//
//     burxt build tests/guarantees.bx -o star-guarantees
//     ./star-guarantees
//
// **The accepting case runs first and its failure is fatal.** A suite of nothing but refusals is
// satisfied by a generator that refuses everything, so if the first case fails nothing below it means
// anything. That is not a style point: this file is mostly refusals.
//
// **No rewriting of the generator's output, ever.** An earlier version patched `lib/html.bx` into
// `std/html.bx` before checking, which meant the suite tested a line the generator does not emit — so
// the generator could emit an import that resolves nowhere and every test would still pass. It did
// exactly that. **A test that repairs its input is testing the repair.**
//
// It lived in Burxt's `tests/runner.rs` while star-burxt lived in Burxt's `lib/`. It moves with the
// code, because a suite that tests a package the repository no longer contains is testing something
// it cannot see change.
//
// ---- and it was `test.py`, in the root ------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Andre's directive, and it is the reason this file exists: *"I will not accept a language that really
// depends on another language. We worked so hard to develop Burxt until it is self hosting."* A file
// named `test.py` in the root of a self-hosting framework is the first thing a visitor sees, and they
// draw the obvious conclusion from it. `README.md` had said for a while that this should not stay
// Python; a directive is what moved it.
//
// Nothing here needed Python: write a file, run a binary, look for a string in its output.
//
// **The port was finished by diffing the two suites, not by reading them.** All 148 cases ran in both
// languages side by side, and the check was that the two printed the same 148 names in the same order
// with the same verdicts — which caught three names the first twelve cases had quietly shortened.
// Then eight defects were planted, one in each assertion written as a NEGATIVE (`!contains`, `== 0`),
// because that is the shape that passes when the stream it reads is empty; exactly those eight failed.
// The two regexes this file used to hold are gone — Burxt has none — and the hand-rolled scans that
// replaced them answer the numbers the regexes did, 52 refusal sites and 3 of them showing a fence.
//
// ---- every literal brace in this file is escaped, and that is not a style choice -----------------
//
// Burxt interpolates `{expr}` inside a string literal, and these fixtures are almost nothing but
// braces — `{{ to_string(x) }}` in a slot, `{ return count + 1; }` in a handler, `class Model { … }`
// in a `===bx` section. So every one of them is written `\{` and `\}`. A forty-brace document escaped
// by hand is exactly where this goes wrong quietly, so the fixtures below were converted
// mechanically and then checked, character for character, against the Python they came from.
//
// The other consequence: a fixture lives in a `const` rather than at the call site. A top-level `let`
// is invisible to a function body, and four copies of the same document is four chances for one of
// them to drift — which is what the first version of this file had.
use "std/files.bx";
use "std/string.bx";
use "std/os.bx";

pure function contains(haystack: String, needle: String) -> Bool {
    return string_find(haystack, needle) >= 0;
}

// `a.index(x) < a.index(y)` in the Python, which raises when a needle is missing. Here a missing
// needle answers `false`, so "the author's code comes first" cannot pass because neither half was
// emitted at all.
pure function before(text: String, first: String, second: String) -> Bool {
    let a: Int = string_find(text, first);
    let b: Int = string_find(text, second);
    return a >= 0 && b >= 0 && a < b;
}

// **Returns its verdict rather than pushing to a shared list.** A top-level `let` is invisible to a
// function body and a `const` may not be a list, so the count is accumulated by the caller — which the
// compiler insisted on and which is the better shape anyway: there is one place the total is known.
function check(name: String, ok: Bool, detail: String) -> Int {
    if ok {
        print("  ok    " + name);
        return 0;
    }
    print("  FAIL  " + name);
    let rows: [String] = string_lines(detail);
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(rows) && i < 8 {
        print("      " + rows[i]);
        i += 1;
    }
    return 1;
}

// The accepting document, and the one five of the cases below read. It is a `const` because a
// top-level `let` is invisible to a function body — and because four copies of a fixture is four
// chances for one of them to drift, which is what the first version of this file had.
const DOC_GOOD: String = ":props: count: Int\n:!props:\n\nAt \{\{ to_string(count) \}\}.\n\n:button: on:click=count + 1\nmore\n:!button:\n";
